{"name":"Clematis Green","hex":"#98b652","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"clematis","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any plant of the genus Clematis, vigorous climbing lianas found throughout the temperate zones."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/clematis"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Clematis","blocks":[{"text":"Clematis is a genus of about 380 species within the buttercup family, Ranunculaceae. Their garden hybrids and cultivars have been popular among gardeners, beginning with Clematis 'Jackmanii', a garden staple since 1862. More cultivars are being produced constantly, mainly of Chinese and Japanese origin."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Clematis_%27Nelly_Moser%27.JPG/330px-Clematis_%27Nelly_Moser%27.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clematis"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Large_purple_clematis_flower_with_white_finger_stamens.jpg/500px-Large_purple_clematis_flower_with_white_finger_stamens.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Large_purple_clematis_flower_with_white_finger_stamens.jpg/1280px-Large_purple_clematis_flower_with_white_finger_stamens.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Large_purple_clematis_flower_with_white_finger_stamens.jpg/500px-Large_purple_clematis_flower_with_white_finger_stamens.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Large, dark purple clematis flower with white finger stamens in sunlight","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALarge_purple_clematis_flower_with_white_finger_stamens.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/PurpleClematis1.jpeg/500px-PurpleClematis1.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/PurpleClematis1.jpeg/1280px-PurpleClematis1.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/PurpleClematis1.jpeg/500px-PurpleClematis1.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikiped